@flexiarg operator/standing-dispatch-pattern-for-the-queue
@title Stand Up A Dispatch Pattern For The Remaining Queue
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 3 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-5wkP86 turn-TQqiPc turn-o1rDOX; first proposed for: A mission has a sequence of remaining items being worked via ad-hoc one-off dispatches, and a handoff gap (missing bell-back) just surfaced the cost of ad-hocery.
@why [orchestration/recorded-handoff]

! conclusion: Name and instantiate a dispatch pattern for the queue: fixed sequencing, per-item dispatch as a modeled object, and a return leg that is expected, so its absence is itself a signal.

  + IF:
    The operator asks to 'set up a dispatch pattern' so the queue is worked sequentially and progressively rather than item-by-one-off.

  + HOWEVER:
    Ad-hoc dispatches are flexible but each one re-derives routing and drops return legs; a standing pattern fixes the shape once and makes gaps visible.

  + THEN:
    Name and instantiate a dispatch pattern for the queue: fixed sequencing, per-item dispatch as a modeled object, and a return leg that is expected, so its absence is itself a signal.

  + BECAUSE:
    It generalises recorded-handoff from one dispatch to a standing pattern over a queue — the lifecycle per item stays the same; the pattern adds sequencing and progression across items.
